The error message says the method doesn't override anything from AbstractHandler. Let me check the Jetty 11 API more carefully. 

In Jetty 11, the `AbstractHandler` class is defined as:
```java
public abstract class AbstractHandler implements Handler
```

And the `Handler` interface has:
```java
void handle(String target, Request baseRequest, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException, ServletException;
```

But `AbstractHandler` implements this as:
```java
public void handle(String target, Request baseRequest, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException, ServletException
```

Wait, maybe in Jetty 11, the method returns void, not boolean. Let me check the actual Jetty 11 source code.

Actually, looking at the Jetty 11.0.9 source:
https://github.com/eclipse/jetty.project/blob/jetty-11.0.9/jetty-server/src/main/java/org/eclipse/jetty/server/handler/AbstractHandler.java

```java
public abstract class AbstractHandler implements Handler
{
    //...

    @Override
    public void handle(String target, Request baseRequest, HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException, ServletException
    {
        // default implementation does nothing
    }
    //...
}
```

So in Jetty 11, `AbstractHandler.handle()` returns `void`, not `boolean`! That's the issue. I changed it to return `boolean` but Jetty 11 uses `void`.

